      ],
      "text": "Example:",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-2-s0002",
      "page": 2,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-2-r0002",
        "page-2-r0003",
        "page-2-r0004"
      ],
      "text": "A steady current of 2.5 A is created in a wire for 1.0 minute. How much charge passed by any given point, and how many electrons would this be?",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-2-s0003",
      "normalized_math": "I = \\frac{Q}{\\Delta t} \\rightarrow I = \\frac{2.5\\,\\mathrm{C}}{\\mathrm{s}} = \\frac{Q}{t}",
      "page": 2,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-2-r0005"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "I equals Q divided by delta t, which leads to I equals 2.5 coulombs per second, equal to Q divided by t.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-2-s0004",
      "normalized_math": "Q = I t = \\left(2.5\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{C}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right)(60\\,\\mathrm{s}) = 150\\,\\mathrm{C}",
      "page": 2,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-2-r0006"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Q equals I times t, equal to 2.5 coulombs per second times 60 seconds, equal to 150 coulombs.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-2-s0005",
      "page": 2,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-2-r0007"
      ],
      "text": "# of electrons:",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-2-s0006",
      "normalized_math": "N = (150\\,\\mathrm{C})\\left(\\frac{1\\,e^-}{1.60\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm{C}}\\right) = 9.4\\times10^{20}\\ \\mathrm{electrons}",
      "page": 2,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-2-r0008"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "N equals 150 coulombs times one electron per 1.60 times 10 to the negative 19 coulombs, equal to 9.4 times 10 to the 20 electrons.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-2-s0007",
      "page": 2,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-2-r0009"
      ],
      "text": "In moles:",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-2-s0008",
      "normalized_math": "n = 9.4\\times10^{20}\\ \\mathrm{electrons}\\left(\\frac{1}{6.022\\times10^{23}\\ \\mathrm{electrons/mol}}\\right) = 0.0016\\,\\mathrm{mol}",
      "page": 2,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-2-r0010"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "n equals 9.4 times 10 to the 20 electrons times one divided by 6.022 times 10 to the 23 electrons per mole, equal to 0.0016 mole.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-2-s0009",
      "level": 1,
      "page": 2,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-2-r0011"
      ],
      "text": "Electrical Resistance",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "complex",
      "detailed_figure_description": "A closed rectangular circuit contains a battery labeled V on the lower left and a zigzag resistor labeled R on the right. Multiple e-minus labels and arrowheads around the wire indicate electron motion: rightward along the bottom, upward on the right, leftward along the top, and downward on the left, forming a counterclockwise path.",
      "figure_alternative": "Circuit with a battery V, resistor R, and counterclockwise electron-flow arrows.",
      "id": "page-2-s0010",
